{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For task_id 101 (water solubility) in successful edits, insert operations yield larger target_delta improvements than replace operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare target_delta distributions for insert vs replace edits in task 101 of train.parquet and test if insert operations produce significantly higher improvements.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet.  \n2. Filter to rows where task_id==101 and target_delta is not null.  \n3. Split into two groups by action_type: 'insert' and 'replace'.  \n4. Compute summary statistics (mean, median, IQR) of target_delta for each group.  \n5. Check distribution normality (e.g., Shapiro–Wilk) and then perform an appropriate test (e.g., two‐sample t‐test or Mann–Whitney U test) to compare insert vs replace target_delta.  \n6. Visualize with side‐by‐side boxplots or violin plots of target_delta by action_type for task 101.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary statistics table for insert and replace in task 101.  \n2. Normality test results and test statistic plus p‐value comparing the two groups.  \n3. Boxplot or violin plot showing target_delta distributions by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For the drug-likeness task (task_id 103), the murcko_scaffold_retained rate is higher in successful examples than in unsuccessful examples.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether successful edits preserve the Murcko scaffold more often than unsuccessful edits in task 103.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.  \n2. Filter each dataset to rows where task_id==103 and murcko_scaffold_retained is not null.  \n3. For each dataset, count retained (True) vs lost (False) scaffold.  \n4. Build a 2×2 contingency table: success vs failure × retained vs lost.  \n5. Apply chi‐squared test of independence (or Fisher’s exact test) to assess significance.  \n6. Create a bar chart of retention rates for successful vs unsuccessful examples.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table with counts and retention rates.  \n2. Chi‐squared statistic and p‐value (or Fisher’s exact test result).  \n3. Bar chart comparing scaffold retention rates."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, edits with very large MW_delta (above the 75th percentile) yield smaller median target_delta improvements than edits with MW_delta in the interquartile range.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Examine whether excessive increases in molecular weight are associated with diminishing target property gains.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null MW_delta or null target_delta.  \n2. Compute the 25th and 75th percentiles of MW_delta.  \n3. Categorize each edit into: low (MW_delta < 25th), medium (25th ≤ MW_delta ≤ 75th), high (MW_delta > 75th).  \n4. Focus on medium vs high groups.  \n5. Compute summary statistics (median, IQR) of target_delta for each group.  \n6. Use a Mann–Whitney U test to compare target_delta between medium and high MW_delta groups.  \n7. Visualize the distribution of target_delta across the three MW_delta categories using boxplots.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Percentile thresholds for MW_delta.  \n2. Summary statistics of target_delta in medium vs high MW_delta groups.  \n3. Mann–Whitney U test statistic and p‐value.  \n4. Boxplot of target_delta by MW_delta category."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, the correlation between QED_delta and target_delta is stronger when the Murcko scaffold is retained than when it is not.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess how preserving the core scaffold affects the relationship between drug-likeness changes and target property improvements.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet; drop rows with null QED_delta or null target_delta or null scaffold flag.  \n2. Split data into two subsets by murcko_scaffold_retained (True vs False).  \n3. For each subset, compute Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients between QED_delta and target_delta.  \n4. Use Fisher’s Z‐transformation to test whether the Pearson correlations differ significantly between the two subsets.  \n5. Plot scatterplots with regression lines for both subsets side by side, annotating correlation coefficients.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Pearson and Spearman correlations and sample sizes for scaffold‐retained vs scaffold‐lost groups.  \n2. Fisher Z‐test statistic and p‐value comparing the two correlations.  \n3. Paired scatterplots with overlayed regression lines and annotations."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits, multi‐step edits (where the 'edit' string contains multiple '>' separators) have a different SA_delta distribution than single‐step edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Investigate whether the complexity of the edit operation impacts changes in synthetic accessibility.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null SA_delta or null edit string.  \n2. For each row, count the number of '>' separators in the edit field: if count>1 label as 'multi‐step', else 'single‐step'.  \n3. Split SA_delta values into two groups by edit complexity.  \n4. Compute summary statistics (mean, median, IQR) for SA_delta in each group.  \n5. Perform a Mann–Whitney U test to compare SA_delta distributions.  \n6. Create side‐by‐side violin plots of SA_delta for single vs multi‐step edits.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Counts of single‐step vs multi‐step edits.  \n2. Summary statistics of SA_delta for both groups.  \n3. Mann–Whitney U test statistic and p‐value.  \n4. Violin plots showing SA_delta distributions by edit complexity."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In unsuccessful edits, for replace operations only, the ratio SA_delta/MW_delta is positively correlated with target_delta, whereas for insert operations it is not.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare how the balance of accessibility versus size change influences target property in different edit types for failures.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet; drop rows with null SA_delta, MW_delta, or target_delta and MW_delta==0.  \n2. Compute a new variable ratio = SA_delta / MW_delta for each row.  \n3. Filter to two subsets: action_type=='replace' and action_type=='insert'.  \n4. For each subset, compute Pearson correlation between ratio and target_delta; record correlation coefficient and p‐value.  \n5. Test significance of each correlation and compare their magnitudes descriptively.  \n6. Plot scatterplots of ratio vs target_delta for replace and insert operations with regression lines.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Sample sizes of replace and insert subsets.  \n2. Pearson correlation coefficients and p‐values for each.  \n3. Scatterplots with regression lines for both action types."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Across both datasets, delete operations have a higher missing‐target_delta rate than insert and replace operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if delete edits are more likely to lack a computed target_delta in both successful and unsuccessful datasets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet.  \n2. For each dataset separately, for each action_type, count total rows and count rows with null target_delta.  \n3. Compute missing rate = null_count / total_count for each action_type in each dataset.  \n4. Build a 2×3 table: dataset (successful, unsuccessful) × action_type (insert, replace, delete) of missing rates.  \n5. Perform a chi‐squared test on the counts of null vs non‐null target_delta by action_type pooled across datasets, or separately for each dataset.  \n6. Visualize missing target_delta rates by action_type and dataset as grouped bar charts.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of missing target_delta rates by action_type for each dataset.  \n2. Chi‐squared test statistic(s) and p‐value(s).  \n3. Grouped bar chart of missing rates."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In unsuccessful edits for task_id 105 (permeability), the variance in QED_delta is greater for delete operations than for insert and replace operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether delete edits produce more variable drug‐likeness changes than other edit types in task 105 failures.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet; filter to task_id==105 and drop rows with null QED_delta.  \n2. Group QED_delta by action_type: 'delete', 'insert', 'replace'.  \n3. Calculate sample variances of QED_delta for each group.  \n4. Perform Levene’s test (or Bartlett’s test) for equal variances across the three groups, focusing on whether delete has significantly higher variance.  \n5. Display group variances in a bar chart with error bars representing group standard deviations or CIs.  \n6. Optionally, show individual group boxplots to visualize spread.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Variance estimates of QED_delta for each action_type in task 105.  \n2. Levene’s test statistic and p‐value.  \n3. Bar chart of group variances and/or boxplots showing spread."  
    }  
  }  
]}
